  "strategy": "### Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to refine the parent solution through phase-based optimization, leveraging stochastic local search and targeted modifications to reduce the correlation integral \\( C \\). The primary motivation is that the parent solution (with n=700) is already strong, and the best improvements often lie through small perturbations. This approach focuses on:\n\n1.  Systematic exploration of peak/trough configurations through structural changes  \n2.  Localized adjustments to optimize finite-lag correlation  \n3.  Multi-resolution testing via interpolation to seed higher-resolution candidates  \n4.  Stochastic reconfiguration to escape local optima  \n\nThe limitations include the inherently discrete nature of sampled functions and the hard constraint on total integral \\( \\sum h_i = n/2 \\). The method precisely addresses the finite-lag correlation without circularity, using standard `np.correlate(mode='full')` calculations scaled by \\( \\Delta x = 2/n \\). It remains within the allowed libraries and should produce valid candidates meeting all constraints.\n\n### Mathematical specifications\n**Representation**:  \n- \\( h = (h_0, h_1, ..., h_{n-1}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\), \\( \\Delta x = 2/n \\)  \n- Integral constraint: \\( \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i \\cdot \\Delta x = 1 \\), which scales to \\( \\sum h_i = n/2 \\)  \n- \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\frac{1}{\\Delta x} \\int_0^2 h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx \\) (computed as \\( \\max(\\text{correlation}) \\cdot \\Delta x \\))  \n\n**Constraints handling**:  \n- Feasibility projection via `project_feasible(h)` in two steps:  \n  1. Clip to [0,1]  \n  2. Balancing linearly while rejecting any change that violates the integral  \n  \\(\\rightarrow\\) Guaranteed \\( h_i \\in [0,1] \\) and \\( \\sum h_i = n/2 \\) at every candidate\n\n**Optimization structure**:\n- Primary formulation: Minimize \\( \\max_l \\Vert \\text{correlation of } h \\text{ and } 1-h \\Vert \\)  \n(this is non-convex and intractable directly)  \n\n**Derived formulation** (core algorithm):  \n1. Evaluate candidate \\( h \\) with `compute_c5`  \n   - \\( c = \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\\text{'full'}) \\)  \n   - \\( C = \\frac{1}{\\Delta x} \\cdot \\max_c \\)  \n\n### Implementation procedure (formal steps)\n**Phase1: Parent preparation**  \n- Validate `initial_h_values` at start if available  \n- Scale parent to satisfy integral: \\( n' = n_{\\text{parent}} \\), \\( \\sum h_i = n'/2 \\)  \n- `check_parent = project_feasible(h_init)`  \n\n**Phase2: Stochastic phase** (default parameters)  \n1. Initialize \\( h_0 = \\overline{\\text{project_feasible}}(\\text{nearest neighbor mutation}) \\)  \n    - Mutation: Swap \u00b11 between peaks/valleys  \n2. For \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( T_{\\text{total}} \\):  \n    a. Sample global adjustment:  \n        - Probability \\( p_\\text{global}=0.2 \\): Morph-scale peaks/sharps  \n        - Probability \\( p_\\text{local}=0.8 \\): Targeted spill-over  \n    b. Apply adjustment to current best \\( h_t \\)  \n    c. Repair using `project_feasible`  \n    d. Evaluate \\( C \\) via `compute_c5`\n\n**Phase3: Amplitude phase**  \n1. Target \u00b10.1 deviation from current \\( C \\)  \n2. While \\( t < T_\\text{final} \\) and \\( \\Delta C > \\text{acceptable} \\):  \n    a. Change one spike with \\( h_i \\) between 0.2 and 0.9  \n    b. Repair and re-evaluate  \n\n**Phase4: Multi-scale boost**  \n1. Downsample \\( h \\) to n=100  \n2. Recombine structure + noise  \n3. Upsample via interpolation + repair  \n\n**Phase5: Final polish**  \n1. Apply \\( 30 \\) global optimizers  \n2. Evaluate \\( C \\) each time  \n\n**Termination**:  \n- Return best \\( h \\) with \\( \\lfloor \\min(C) \\rfloor \\)  \n\n### Search choices and hypertuning\n1. Mutation: Use bit-wise mutation at peaks and valleys  \n2. Local repair: Replace single points in valleys with interpolations  \n3. Hypertuning: Vary \\( T_\\text{total} \\) to 100*s via budget clock  \n\n### Computational budget and robustness  \n**Dominant cost**:  \n- Iterative phase (nsample scale)  \n- Worst: \\( O(n \\log n) \\) per evaluation with n=1000\n\n**Scheduler**:  \n- Check budget every 30 steps  \n- Exit if \\( \\Delta t > 1100 - \\text{elapsed} \\)  \n\n**Validation**:  \n- At end: Enforce h bounds, integral, then evaluate \\( C \\) again  \n- Check \\( C \\) is finite (\u22650)  \n\n**Fallback**:  \n- Preserve parents from earlier phases  \n\n### Final return specification  \n**Interface**: Return `(h, C, n)` where \\( \\min(C) < 0.380 \\)  \n`h` satisfied `project_feasible` checks",
